var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-minerva"]=(()=>{var n=Object.defineProperty;var s=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(a,e)=>{for(var o in e)n(a,o,{get:e[o],enumerable:!0})},u=(a,e,o,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let t of h(e))!d.call(a,t)&&t!==o&&n(a,t,{get:()=>e[t],enumerable:!(i=s(e,t))||i.enumerable});return a};var c=a=>u(n({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),a);var m={};l(m,{journey:()=>r});var r={id:"deity-minerva",kind:"deity",paths:["roman"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/minerva",title:"Minerva of a Thousand Works",summary:"A month with Minerva, Roman goddess of skill, craft, learning and medicine: the Capitoline triad, the Quinquatrus of March, and a practice of patient, dedicated work.",welcome:`This journey runs about a lunar month, and it happens at your desk or workbench as much as at a shrine. You will learn who Minerva was to the Romans, how she differs from Greek Athena, how craftspeople, students, doctors and teachers honoured her at the Quinquatrus, and how people keep her today. You will dedicate nine sessions of real work to her, and at the end you may make a self-dedication.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation, and it does not make you a priest. It is a personal commitment to a goddess who values effort, care and skill earned slowly.`,about:`Minerva is the Roman goddess of skilled work and the mind: weaving and spinning, carpentry and shoemaking, medicine, teaching, poetry, painting, and the strategy of war. She came to Rome from the Etruscans, whose Menrva was already a major goddess, and she stood beside Jupiter and Juno in the great Capitoline temple. Her chief festival, the Quinquatrus of 19 March, belonged to the working people of Rome: Ovid calls her "goddess of a thousand works". Greek influence made her Athena, so she gained Athena's birth from her father's head, her owl and her olive, and her stories such as Arachne. Modern Roman polytheists honour her at the Quinquatrus and through dedicated study and craft; many students, makers and healers keep her as a patron.`,notes:['Minerva honours skill earned through effort. She is a poor choice of patron for shortcuts, cheating or "manifesting" exam results without study.',"Most of her famous myths (her birth from Jupiter's head, Arachne, Medusa) are Greek stories of Athena retold by Latin poets. The journey separates these from Roman cult, and is honest about the troubling ones.","In Roman Britain she was joined with the local goddess Sulis at Bath, where many curse tablets were left. This app does not make curses; the journey explains what they were.","Modern Roman polytheism is small and varied, and this app is inclusive. Minerva's worshippers were the working people of every trade, and her worship is open to anyone."],holyDays:[],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"At the workbench",intro:"Begin where Minerva lives: at your work. This opening rite greets her in Roman form at your desk, table or workbench, and dedicates a first small piece of work to her.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"minerva-tidy",title:"Clear the workspace",instructions:"On each of these two days, clean and order the place where you study or make things. Put tools where they belong. Throw out what is broken. A clean bench is a first offering to a craft goddess.",times:2,every:"day",prompt:"What does my workspace say about how I treat my own skill?"}],rite:{title:"Greeting Minerva",summary:"Purification, Janus first, then incense, oil and a short piece of real work offered to Minerva.",minutes:30,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of clean water and a towel",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A head covering"},{name:"Frankincense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"An incense burner",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner"},{name:"A little olive oil in a small dish",ref:"traditional/vessel/oil_bottle"},{name:"A lamp or blue candle",ref:"traditional/candle/blue"},{name:"The tools of your work: pen and notebook, needle and thread, brushes, a textbook"},{name:"An owl image or olive sprig",optional:!0}],preparation:"Clear your workspace. Choose one small task you can do well in about fifteen minutes: a page of notes, a few rows of knitting, a sketch, a short practice session. Read through the words until you can say them without stumbling.",steps:[{title:"Wash and veil",instructions:"Wash your hands and dry them. Draw a fold of cloth over your head, as Romans did to pray."},{title:"Janus first",instructions:"Light the incense and give a few grains to Janus, opener of every prayer.",spoken:"Father Janus, open the way for my prayer."},{title:"Light and call",instructions:"Light the lamp or candle. Give more incense, and raise your hands, palms up.",spoken:`Minerva, goddess of a thousand works,
mistress of craft and learning, of healing and good counsel,
or by whatever name it is right to call you:
be honoured by this incense.
I come to learn who you are.`,light:!0},{title:"Oil",instructions:"Touch a drop of olive oil to your fingertips and then to your main tool: the pen, the needle, the brush.",spoken:"Be honoured by this oil. Be willing and favourable to my hands and my mind."},{title:"Work",instructions:"Do the task you chose, carefully and without hurry. This is the offering.",minutes:15},{title:"Offer the work",instructions:"Lay the finished piece before the lamp.",spoken:"Minerva, this is my work, small as it is. Accept it."},{title:"Vesta last, and turn",instructions:"Name Vesta, turn to your right, uncover your head and put out the flame.",spoken:"Vesta of the hearth, be honoured, last as always.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Keep the piece of work on or near your shrine for the month. Put the leftover oil on the earth or into a plant pot.",safety:"Never leave the lamp, candle or incense unattended; keep them well away from papers, fabric and art materials. Keep incense smoke away from birds and people with asthma. Oil on fabric or paper is flammable; wipe up spills."}},{kind:"study",title:"Goddess of a thousand works",intro:"Five lessons, about two days each: who Minerva is; her temples and the Capitoline triad; the Quinquatrus and the trades; how people honour her today; and the Greek stories and other hard parts.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"minerva-who",title:"Who Minerva is",summary:"An Etruscan-Italian goddess of skill and mind, and how she differs from Athena.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"From Menrva to Minerva",body:`Minerva reached Rome from the Etruscans, the people to the north whose kings once ruled the city. Their goddess Menrva appears on bronze mirrors and in temples, often armed and often present at the birth of heroes. The Romans, and other Italians, took her up early: by the time of the Republic she was one of the three gods of the Capitol.

Romans connected her name with mens, mind, and with memory. Modern linguists think that is probably right, at least in part. She is the goddess of the skilled mind and the skilled hand together: thinking well and making well.`},{heading:"What she looks after",body:`Ovid gives the best list of her people, in his poem on the Roman calendar. Girls learning to spin and weave. Fullers, who cleaned cloth, and dyers. Shoemakers, however skilled. Doctors, "who drive out disease by Phoebus's art". Schoolmasters, "a crowd cheated of their pay", he jokes. Painters, sculptors and poets. And he sums it up: "mille dea est operum", "she is the goddess of a thousand works" (Fasti 3.815\u2013833).

Minerva was also a goddess of war, especially of strategy and defence, and she appears helmeted, with spear, shield and the aegis, a goatskin cape with the Gorgon's head. In early Roman cult the craft side seems to have been stronger; the warrior side grew as she was matched with Athena.`},{heading:"Not just Athena",body:`From the third century BC Romans equated their gods with Greek ones, a process scholars call interpretatio graeca. Minerva became Athena, and gained Athena's myths: born fully armed from her father's head, the contest with Poseidon for Athens, the owl and the olive, Arachne, Medusa, the Trojan Palladium.

Much of this is a good fit, since both goddesses are virgin patrons of craft, wisdom and defence. But the Roman Minerva was worshipped through Roman forms, Roman festivals and Roman trades. If you also honour Athena, give each her own setting and rites. Hellenic polytheists and Roman polytheists approach them differently, and both deserve respect.`},{heading:"Her signs",body:'The owl is her bird, from Athena, and Roman art uses it too. Her plant is the olive, also from Athena; olive oil fuelled the lamps by which Romans worked late (the word "lucubration", working at night by lamplight, comes from that). Her tools are the spindle and loom, the pen and the scalpel. Her colours in modern practice are blue, grey and silver. Her animal in some Roman contexts is the snake, guardian and healer.'}],knowAndDont:{known:"Etruscan Menrva is well attested on mirrors and in inscriptions. Ovid's list of her worshippers (Fasti 3.815\u2013834) survives exactly. Her place on the Capitol and her identification with Athena are certain.",unknown:"The exact etymology of her name is uncertain. How warlike the early Roman Minerva was, before Greek influence, is debated. Many of her popular symbols (the owl, the olive) are really Athena's."},inPractice:'Write down your "thousand works": every skill you practise, from cooking to coding. Put the list on her shrine. She is the patron of all of them.',reflect:["Which of my skills am I proudest of, and which do I neglect?","Do I want Minerva, Athena, or both, and how will I keep them distinct?"],further:["Ovid, Fasti 3.809\u2013848 (Latin at The Latin Library; Riley's 1851 prose translation is public domain)","Robert Turcan, The Gods of Ancient Rome (2000)"],library:["traditional/deity/minerva","traditional/deity/athena"]},{id:"minerva-temples",title:"The Capitol, the Aventine and Minerva Capta",summary:"Where Minerva was worshipped, and how her temples tied her to Rome's guilds, healers and safety.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The triad",body:`In the great temple on the Capitoline Hill, dedicated by tradition in 509 BC, Minerva had her own chamber beside Jupiter Optimus Maximus and Juno Regina. This is the Capitoline triad, copied in towns across the empire. At the Feast of Jupiter (Epulum Iovis) on the Ides of September and November, the three were served a banquet: Jupiter on a couch, Juno and Minerva on chairs.

So Minerva was not only a goddess of workshops. She was one of the three powers on whom Rome's safety rested, the wise one among them.`},{heading:"The guild temple",body:`Minerva had a temple on the Aventine Hill that became the meeting place of a guild of writers and actors. In 207 BC the poet Livius Andronicus, a freed slave from southern Italy who had translated Homer into Latin and written a hymn for the state, was honoured by the senate, and his fellow scribes and actors were given the right to meet at Minerva's temple. Craft guilds (collegia) of many trades kept Minerva as their patron.

That is worth sitting with. One of Rome's first poets was a formerly enslaved foreigner, and his goddess became the patron of everyone who made things with words.`},{heading:"Captured Minerva",body:`On the slope of the Caelian Hill stood a small shrine of Minerva Capta, "Captured Minerva". Ovid wonders about the name (Fasti 3.835\u2013848): perhaps because "capitale" means quick-witted; perhaps because she sprang from her father's head (caput); perhaps because her image was brought captive from Falerii, conquered in 241 BC. The last is the likeliest. Like Juno Regina from Veii, she is a reminder that Rome took gods as well as land.`},{heading:"Minerva the healer, and the Palladium",body:`Minerva Medica, the healer, had a shrine on the Esquiline, and votive offerings found at her sanctuaries in Italy include clay models of body parts, given in hope of healing or thanks for it. Doctors were among her worshippers, as Ovid says.

Romans also believed that the Palladium, a small ancient wooden image of Pallas Athena/Minerva rescued from Troy by Aeneas, was kept hidden in the temple of Vesta, and that Rome would stand as long as it was safe. No one but the Vestals saw it. Whether it existed we cannot know; that Romans believed in it tells you how central Minerva was to their sense of safety.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Capitoline triad and the Epulum Iovis are well attested. Festus records the honour to Livius Andronicus and the guild at Minerva's Aventine temple; Livy (27.37) records his hymn. Ovid discusses Minerva Capta. Votive body-part offerings from Italian healing sanctuaries survive in quantity.",unknown:"The 509 BC date for the Capitoline temple is Roman tradition. Whether the Palladium existed is unknowable. The precise organisation of the writers' guild is unclear."},inPractice:"If you belong to a profession or craft, find out its traditions and its patron customs, ancient or modern. Consider giving something to a trade school, library or apprenticeship fund in Minerva's name.",reflect:["Who taught me my skills, and how could I honour them?","What does it mean that the craft goddess was also one of the city's protectors?"],further:["Mary Beard, John North and Simon Price, Religions of Rome (1998)","Livy, History of Rome 27.37 (public-domain translations on Gutenberg)"],library:["traditional/deity/minerva","traditional/deity/jupiter","traditional/deity/juno","traditional/deity/vesta"]},{id:"minerva-quinquatrus",title:"The Quinquatrus and the trades",summary:"The March festival of artisans, students and doctors, the June festival of flute-players, and the manner of offering.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Five days in March",body:`The Quinquatrus began on 19 March. The name means the "fifth day" after the Ides, but by Ovid's time it had grown into a five-day festival, 19 to 23 March. Ovid explains: "sanguine prima vacat, nec fas concurrere ferro: causa, quod est illa nata Minerva die", "the first day is free of blood, and it is not right to clash with iron, because Minerva was born on that day" (Fasti 3.811\u2013812). The next four days had gladiatorial games, for "the warlike goddess delights in drawn swords". The last day, 23 March, was also the Tubilustrium, the purification of the sacred trumpets.

Ovid then turns to the people: "Pallada nunc pueri teneraeque orate puellae; qui bene placarit Pallada, doctus erit", "now, boys and tender girls, pray to Pallas; whoever pleases Pallas well will be learned" (3.815\u2013816).`},{heading:"The festival of the working people",body:`The Quinquatrus was a holiday for craftspeople and schools. Ovid tells weavers, fullers, dyers, shoemakers, doctors, teachers, painters, sculptors and poets to bring her gifts. Schoolchildren had a holiday, and later writers mention the minerval, the fee or gift a pupil brought the teacher at about this time of year.

A modern Quinquatrus keeps that spirit. On 19 March make a bloodless offering of incense, oil and cakes. Dedicate your tools, renew your study or craft, give a gift to a teacher or mentor, and show your work to someone. Over the following days, practise your skill every day.`},{heading:"The lesser Quinquatrus",body:"On 13 June came the Quinquatrus Minusculae, the festival of the tibicines, the flute-players who played at every sacrifice. Ovid and Livy tell how they once went on strike and left for Tibur when their right to feast in Jupiter's temple was taken away; Rome could not sacrifice without them, so they were brought back drunk in carts and their rights restored (Fasti 6.651\u2013692; Livy 9.30). Minerva was said to have invented the flute. Musicians and anyone whose work supports others' rituals can keep this day."},{heading:"How to offer",body:'Minerva receives the standard Roman form: clean hands, covered head, Janus first, her name and titles, the gift named exactly, the request that she be "volens propitia", willing and favourable, "macte esto" as you give it, Vesta last, a turn to the right. Her offerings are incense, olive oil, honey cakes, and above all work: a finished piece, an hour of study, a lesson taught. On the Quinquatrus first day, nothing that involves blood.'}],knowAndDont:{known:"Ovid's Fasti 3.809\u2013834 describes the Quinquatrus and its worshippers in words that survive exactly; Varro (On the Latin Language 6.14) explains the name. The flute-players' strike is in Livy 9.30 and Ovid, Fasti 6.651\u2013692. The Tubilustrium is in the calendars.",unknown:"Varro says the Quinquatrus was originally a single day, and why it grew to five is unclear. The minerval is mentioned by late writers (Tertullian, among others), and its earlier form is uncertain. Whether households made private offerings to Minerva at the Quinquatrus is not recorded."},inPractice:"Put 19\u201323 March and 13 June in your calendar. Prepare now: on 19 March you will dedicate your tools and a period of study or craft to her.",reflect:['Who is my "schoolmaster cheated of his pay", the teacher I owe thanks to?',"What would a bloodless, generous festival of my own work look like?"],further:["Ovid, Fasti 3.809\u2013848 and 6.649\u2013710 (Latin at The Latin Library)","Livy, History of Rome 9.30"],library:["traditional/deity/minerva","traditional/herb/frankincense"],premade:["cosmic-wednesday-mercury-study"]},{id:"minerva-today",title:"Minerva today: study, craft and shrine",summary:"How modern devotees keep her, dedicating work and study, and how to set up her shrine.",minutes:6,sections:[{heading:"Who keeps her now",body:`Roman polytheists today, in groups like Nova Roma or alone, keep the Quinquatrus and often honour Minerva on the Ides with Jupiter as part of the Capitoline triad. Many people outside Roman polytheism, including witches, eclectic pagans and simply people who like her, take Minerva as a patron of study, craft, nursing and medicine. Universities and academies across the world still use her image: she stands on seals and buildings as the emblem of learning.

What ties devotees together is the idea that work itself is worship. You honour Minerva most by doing your work carefully and getting better at it.`},{heading:"Dedicating work",body:`The core practice is dedication. Before a study session, a shift, a project or a piece of making, pause, light a lamp, give a grain of incense, and say: "Minerva, I dedicate this work to you. Be willing and favourable to my hands and mind." Then work, with full attention. At the end, thank her and show her what you did.

Romans dedicated things to the gods by placing them in temples: tools, weapons, models, written verses. You can do the same in miniature: a first finished piece, a sample of your craft, a printed essay laid on her shrine for a while.`},{heading:"Setting up her shrine",body:`Put it near where you work: a corner of your desk, a shelf above the workbench. A blue or grey cloth. An owl figure, an olive sprig, or a small image of the helmeted goddess. A lamp or candle in a stable holder, kept well away from papers and fabric. An incense burner. A small dish of olive oil. A sample of your work, and the tools you use most.

Keep it tidy. Minerva is honoured by order: a cluttered, dusty shrine beside a neglected desk says more than any prayer. If space is short, a single shelf with a lamp and an owl is enough.`},{heading:"Minerva for healers and carers",body:`Because Ovid names doctors among her worshippers and Minerva Medica had her own shrine, many nurses, doctors, therapists and carers keep her today. A simple practice: before a shift, touch the shrine or say a line ("Minerva Medica, steady my hands and clear my mind"), and afterwards, give a grain of incense in thanks for the work done. Romans left clay models of healed limbs and organs at healing shrines; a modern equivalent is a written note of thanks, or a donation to a hospital or medical charity.

Minerva's help in healing comes through skill and knowledge. Never treat prayer as a substitute for proper training or medical care, for yourself or for others.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Modern groups publish their calendars and the Quinquatrus is widely kept. Ancient Romans dedicated tools, models and verses in temples, as surviving dedications and Latin poems show.",unknown:'There is no ancient evidence for a "study ritual" of the modern kind, though prayers to Minerva for learning appear in literature. The modern practice of dedicating each session is a natural extension of Roman habits, not a documented rite.'},inPractice:"Set up her shrine this week. Choose a regular session (daily study, weekly craft night) and begin dedicating it to her.",reflect:["What would it change to treat my work as an offering?","Where does her shrine belong in my home?"],further:["Robert Turcan, The Gods of Ancient Rome (2000)","Nova Roma's published religious calendar (novaroma.org)"],library:["traditional/tool/altar","traditional/candle/blue","traditional/crystal/fluorite","traditional/crystal/sodalite"],premade:["kitchen-solitary-book-of-shadows-blessing"]},{id:"minerva-hard",title:"Arachne, Medusa and the tablets of Bath",summary:"The troubling Greek stories, the curse tablets of Sulis Minerva, and what not to ask of her.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Arachne",body:`In Ovid's Metamorphoses (book 6), the young weaver Arachne boasts that she is better than Minerva. In a contest, she weaves the crimes of the gods against women; her work is flawless. Minerva, furious, tears it up and strikes her, and Arachne hangs herself; the goddess turns her into a spider. The story is Greek in origin and told with sympathy for Arachne.

Modern readers take it different ways: a warning against arrogance, a story about a goddess who cannot bear to be outdone, or a protest by an artist against the gods. You do not have to resolve it. You can notice that Minerva is a goddess of high standards, and that pride in your skill is fine, as long as it does not turn into contempt for others.`},{heading:"Medusa",body:`In Ovid (Metamorphoses 4.794\u2013803), Medusa was a beautiful girl raped by Neptune in Minerva's temple; Minerva, offended at the desecration, turned Medusa's hair to snakes. Many modern readers find this deeply unjust, punishing the victim. Earlier Greek versions differ, and Ovid's is not the only one.

This is Latin poetry built on Greek myth, not Roman cult, and you need not accept it as a picture of the goddess. Some modern devotees see Medusa's head on Minerva's aegis as a protective power for those who have been harmed. That is a modern reading, but a widely shared one.`},{heading:"Sulis Minerva and the curse tablets",body:`At Bath in Roman Britain, the hot springs of the local goddess Sulis were joined to Minerva: the temple was dedicated to Sulis Minerva. Archaeologists have recovered well over a hundred lead tablets from the spring, mostly asking the goddess to punish thieves who had stolen cloaks, rings or money, often until the stolen thing was returned.

They are a remarkable record of ordinary people's religion, and they show the Roman pattern of local gods merging with Roman ones (interpretatio romana, the mirror image of interpretatio graeca). This app does not make curse tablets or curses of any kind. If you have been wronged, ask Minerva for clear thinking, good counsel and justice through proper means.`},{heading:"What not to ask",body:"Do not ask Minerva to let you pass without studying, to win by cheating, or to harm a rival. She is approached for skill and wisdom, and she answers through your own effort. Do not offer her sloppy work as if it were your best. And do not rush: Minerva's gifts come through practice repeated over months and years."}],knowAndDont:{known:"Ovid tells the Arachne (Metamorphoses 6.1\u2013145) and Medusa (4.794\u2013803) stories. The Bath tablets have been published by R. S. O. Tomlin and are on display at the Roman Baths museum.",unknown:"How far Romans connected Ovid's Minerva with the goddess of their workshops is unclear. The Medusa-as-protector reading is modern. Exactly how Sulis and Minerva were understood together at Bath is debated."},inPractice:"Choose a skill where you have been tempted to cut corners. Dedicate one honest, patient session to it this week.",reflect:["What do I make of Arachne: warning, protest, or both?","Where in my work am I proud, and where am I arrogant?"],further:["Ovid, Metamorphoses books 4 and 6 (public-domain translations on Gutenberg)","R. S. O. Tomlin, Tabellae Sulis: Roman Inscribed Tablets of Tin and Lead from the Sacred Spring at Bath (1988)"],library:["traditional/deity/minerva","traditional/deity/athena"]}],practices:[{id:"minerva-daily-practice",title:"Fifteen minutes",instructions:"Each day of the study stage, give fifteen focused minutes to one skill you want to improve, dedicated to Minerva. No phone, no multitasking.",times:10,every:"day",prompt:"What did I practise, and what did I learn by doing it?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine sessions of dedicated work",intro:"Nine sessions of real work, each opened and closed in the Roman manner. Nine is a frame for the practice, not a claim about ancient custom; the point is to build the habit of dedicating your work.",days:9,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"minerva-nine-sessions",title:"Opening and closing the work",instructions:`Choose one project for all nine sessions: a course, a piece of craft, a skill. Each session: wash your hands and cover your head. A grain of incense to Janus. Light Minerva's lamp, give incense, and say: "Minerva, goddess of a thousand works, I dedicate this work to you. Be willing and favourable to my hands and mind." Uncover your head and work for at least thirty minutes. At the end, lay what you did before the lamp: "Minerva, be honoured by this work." Name Vesta, turn to your right, and put out the flame.`,times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What did I make or learn? Where did I hurry, and where did I take care?"},{id:"minerva-teach",title:"Teach someone",instructions:"Once in the nine days, teach someone something you know: show a friend a recipe, explain a concept, help a younger person with homework. Ovid includes teachers among her worshippers. Offer the lesson to her.",times:1,prompt:"What did teaching show me about what I know?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Weighing it with a clear head",intro:"A few days to think it through, as Minerva would. Stopping, pausing, or choosing another goddess or path is a good outcome: good judgement is her gift, and using it is honouring her.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"minerva-discern",title:"A clear-headed review",instructions:"Reread your notes from the nine sessions. Then answer the questions below, a few each evening, as if writing a careful report.",times:3,every:"day",prompt:"What does the evidence of this month say about me and Minerva?"}],questions:["Did dedicating my work to her change the work, or how I felt about it?","Can I keep a regular practice of dedicated work, and the Quinquatrus, for a year?",'Am I drawn to Minerva, or to Athena, or to a general "wisdom goddess"? They overlap but are not identical.',"How do I feel about the Greek stories (Arachne, Medusa), and can I honour her with those in view?","Am I hoping for shortcuts? If so, is she the right patron?","Would I rather keep working with her informally, without a dedication? That is a sound choice."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The dedication",intro:"If you go ahead, hold this at your shrine and workspace, ideally on 19 March, the first day of the Quinquatrus, or on an Ides. It is a self-dedication, not an initiation, and does not make you a priest. It is a commitment to a goddess of skill, made in exact words and sealed with a piece of your own work.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Minerva",summary:"Purification, Janus first, a prayer to Minerva by her titles, a spoken dedication, tools anointed with oil, a finished piece of work and honey cakes offered, and Vesta last.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"A bowl of clean water and a towel",ref:"traditional/vessel/bowl"},{name:"A head covering"},{name:"Frankincense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"An incense burner",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner"},{name:"Olive oil in a small dish",ref:"traditional/vessel/oil_bottle"},{name:"A lamp or blue candle",ref:"traditional/candle/blue"},{name:"Honey cakes or a libum"},{name:"An offering dish",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"Your main tools"},{name:"A finished piece of your work, the best you can make"},{name:"Your written dedication"}],preparation:"In the days before, finish one piece of work to the best of your ability: an essay, a garment, a drawing, a recording, a dish. Bake honey cakes or a libum (about 250 g ricotta, 100 g flour, one egg, baked on bay leaves at 200\xB0C for 35\u201340 minutes, then drizzled with honey). Write your dedication: who you are, that you dedicate yourself to Minerva as a devotee, for how long (a year is wise), and what you will do (dedicated work, the Quinquatrus, a skill you will practise, someone you will teach). Clean your workspace. Practise reading the words aloud.",steps:[{title:"Purify and veil",instructions:"Wash your hands and face. Put on clean clothes. Cover your head."},{title:"Janus first",instructions:"Give incense to Janus.",spoken:`Father Janus, first in every prayer,
open the way to Minerva.`},{title:"Invoke Minerva",instructions:"Light the lamp, give incense, raise your hands with palms up.",spoken:`Minerva, goddess of a thousand works,
of the Capitol with Jupiter and Juno,
of weavers and healers, teachers and makers,
of good counsel and clear thought,
or by whatever name it is right to call you:
I offer you this incense, and I pray good prayers,
that you may be willing and favourable
to me, my work and my household.`,light:!0},{title:"Speak the dedication",instructions:"Read your dedication aloud, clearly. If you stumble, stop and begin again.",minutes:5},{title:"Anoint the tools",instructions:"Touch a drop of olive oil to each of your main tools.",spoken:`Minerva, these are the tools of my work.
Guide my hands in their use.`},{title:"Offer the work and the cakes",instructions:"Lay the finished piece of work before the lamp. Set a honey cake on the dish beside it.",spoken:`Minerva, be honoured by this cake,
and by this work, the best I can make.
As I have spoken, so I will do.`},{title:"Silence",instructions:"Sit with the lamp and your work.",minutes:5,timed:!0},{title:"Vesta last, and turn",instructions:"Name Vesta, turn to your right, uncover your head and put out the flame.",spoken:"Vesta of the hearth, be honoured, last as always. It is done.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Leave the work on her shrine for at least nine days. Share the rest of the cakes with someone who taught you, or whom you teach. Give the god's portion to the earth the next day. Begin a new piece of work within the week.",safety:"Never leave the lamp, candle or incense unattended; keep them well away from paper, fabric and art materials. Oil is flammable. The cakes contain dairy, egg, gluten and honey; do not give honey to infants under one."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"A year of work",intro:"Minerva's ongoing worship is your work itself, kept steady through the year, with the Quinquatrus as its high point.",days:4,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"minerva-weekly",title:"Weekly dedicated session",instructions:"Once a week, open a work or study session in the Roman manner and dedicate it to her, as in the nine sessions.",times:52,every:"week",prompt:"What did I build this week?"},{id:"minerva-ides",title:"The Ides with the triad",instructions:"On each Ides (13th, or 15th in March, May, July, October), make a short offering of incense to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva together, the Capitoline triad, with Janus first and Vesta last.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"What wisdom did this month ask of me?"},{id:"minerva-year",title:"The yearly round",instructions:"Keep the Quinquatrus, 19\u201323 March: on the 19th a bloodless offering of incense, oil and honey cakes, tools dedicated, a gift to a teacher; on the following days, daily practice of your craft. Keep 13 June, the lesser Quinquatrus, with music. Rededicate each year on 19 March: read your dedication, show her the year's best work, and set the next year's skill.",times:1,prompt:"How has my skill grown this year, and what will I learn next?"}]}],sources:['Ovid, Fasti 3.809\u2013848, incl. 3.811\u2013812, 3.815\u2013816 and 3.833 "mille dea est operum" (Latin, The Latin Library: https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti3.shtml); translations original',"Ovid, Fasti 6.651\u2013692, the flute-players (Latin, The Latin Library: https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti6.shtml) (paraphrased)","Ovid, Metamorphoses 4.794\u2013803 and 6.1\u2013145 (paraphrased)","Livy, History of Rome 9.30 and 27.37; Varro, On the Latin Language 6.14; Festus on Livius Andronicus (paraphrased)","R. S. O. Tomlin, Tabellae Sulis (Oxford, 1988)","Robert Turcan, The Gods of Ancient Rome (Edinburgh, 2000)","Mary Beard, John North and Simon Price, Religions of Rome (Cambridge, 1998)"]};return c(m);})();
